  "aboutTitle": "How it works",
  "aboutPipelineTitle": "Signal pipeline",
  "aboutPipelineBody": "1. A face detector locates your face (on Android and iOS). 2. The average colour of skin pixels on your forehead and cheeks is computed for each frame; pixels are discarded immediately. 3. Samples are re-timed onto an even 30 Hz grid. 4. An rPPG algorithm combines the colour channels to cancel lighting and motion effects. 5. The signal is band-pass filtered (0.6–4 Hz) and the strongest frequency between 40 and 180 BPM is found. 6. Quality checks decide whether a value can be reported.",
  "aboutAlgorithmsTitle": "Algorithms",
  "aboutActiveAlgorithm": "Active",
  "aboutFaceDetectionTitle": "Face detection",
  "aboutFaceDetectionMobile": "Android and iOS use Google ML Kit face detection on the device, configured for bounding boxes and head angle only. No recognition, tracking or attribute classification is used.",
  "aboutFaceDetectionWeb": "The web build has no face detector. It checks that skin-coloured pixels fill the on-screen guide, so it cannot confirm that a face is present or that only one person is in view.",
  "aboutQualityTitle": "Signal quality",
  "aboutLimitationsTitle": "Known limitations",
  "aboutLimitationsBody": "Not clinically validated. rPPG is known to be less reliable on darker skin tones, in low or changing light, with movement, and with low-quality cameras. Respiratory rate, blood pressure, oxygen saturation and other vital signs are not estimated.",
  "aboutNeuralTitle": "Neural models",
  "aboutNeuralBody": "Neural rPPG models (for example EfficientPhys and TS-CAN from rPPG-Toolbox) were evaluated but are not included: the available pretrained weights are distributed under a licence with use restrictions and were trained on research-only datasets. See docs/model_evaluation.md in the source repository.",
  "aboutNotices": "View open-source licences",
  "privacyTitle": "Privacy",
  "privacyBody1Title": "Camera",
  "privacyBody1": "Camera access is requested only when you start a measurement. Frames are processed in memory into average colour values and discarded immediately. No video or images are recorded, saved or uploaded. The camera is switched off when a measurement ends, is cancelled, or the app leaves the foreground.",
  "privacyBody2Title": "Stored data",
  "privacyBody2": "Only measurements you choose to save are stored: date, duration, heart-rate estimate, quality metrics and algorithm version. They are stored locally ({backend}) and are not encrypted by the app beyond the protection your operating system or browser provides.",
  "@privacyBody2": {
    "placeholders": {
      "backend": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "privacyBody3Title": "No accounts, analytics or cloud",
  "privacyBody3": "There is no account, no cloud sync, no advertising and no analytics or crash-reporting SDK. The Android release build has no internet permission, and the web build loads all of its code and fonts from its own server.",
  "privacyBody4Title": "Third-party components",
  "privacyBody4": "Face detection on Android and iOS uses Google ML Kit, which runs on the device. On Android the app has no internet permission, so ML Kit cannot send anything. On iOS, Google's ML Kit SDK may send performance and usage metrics to Google under Google's own terms; the app itself sends no data. The web version uses no face detector and makes no third-party requests.",
  "privacyBody5Title": "Export and deletion",
  "privacyBody5": "You can export your measurements as JSON or CSV and delete individual measurements or all data in Settings. Deletion removes the records from the app's database; like most apps, it cannot guarantee physical erasure from flash storage.",
